// Just some example code to play around with using the features that do work for now, I will update this language more in the future

int x = 15;

print(x*2;);

for (int i = 0; i <= x; i++;) {
    print("i = " + i;);
}

print("\n";);

if (x < 15;) {
    print(x+1;);
} else {
    print(x-1;);
}

myFunction("Hello World";); // This should return "Hello World, 10" because in the function definition we set int num to a default value of 10 if omitted
myFunction("Goodbye World"; 101;);

function myFunction(string message; int num = 10;) {
    print(message + ", " + num;);
}

function max(int a; int b;) {
    if (a > b;) {
        return int a;
    } else if (b > a;) {
        return int b;
    } else {
        return null;
    }
}

print(max(3; 6;) + " " + max(7; 1;););

print(max(4; 4;););

double n = -127.001;

print(n;);

/* while (false;) {
    print("While loops are still bugged, using them causes the program to wait indefinetely even if the loop breaks";);
    print("What is even more strange is that in my code, a for loop is just supposed to be syntactic sugar for a while loop, yet the for loops work;";)
}*/

if (int y = 10; y == 10;) {
    print("Isn't this cool how statements can be put in to any control structure";);
}

/* The reason I semicolon separate all things is that in anything, from function parameters to arguments in control structures like 
conditionals is because actually each thing acts as a real runnable statement, a piece of code. (yes, `y == 10;` is a valid piece of code).
This allows for more flexibility, as you saw in my function I could just initialize a paramter to a certain value just like a variable
declaration syntax and it would work the same and provide a default value. Theoretically, you can fit any single statement of code into a 
semicolon separated argument. This also allows for a lot of internal consistency in the language (especially because languages like C++ and 
Java semicolon separate for for loops but nothing else)*/